  ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Cancellation
  ;;
  ;; Until now all five cancels notified NOBODY. A customer was refunded in
  ;; full and an owner lost a payout in silence, including on
  ;; :transition/cancel-from-returned, the damage write-off path.
  ;;
  ;; TEN ENTRIES, TWO TEMPLATES. :on and :to are both single-valued, so
  ;; five transitions x two roles is ten entries and there is no shorter
  ;; spelling. But they share exactly two templates - one per role - and
  ;; that is deliberate:
  ;;
  ;;   1. The five are identical in money terms. Each runs
  ;;      :action/calculate-full-refund + :action/stripe-refund-payment +
  ;;      :action/cancel-booking, all :actor/operator, all into
  ;;      :state/cancelled. For both parties the outcome is the same in all
  ;;      five: customer refunded in full, provider paid nothing. There is
  ;;      no per-transition substance for per-transition copy to carry.
  ;;
  ;;   2. Every string in these templates is hand-entered into Console's
  ;;      content/email-texts.json by the client, in every language. Ten
  ;;      templates would be roughly five times that work for wording that
  ;;      says the same thing.
  ;;
  ;; SO THE COPY IS SITUATION-NEUTRAL ON PURPOSE. Neither template says
  ;; where the garment physically is, because that genuinely differs across
  ;; the five (never collected / with the customer / possibly lost / back at
  ;; the laundry) and one template cannot be right about all four. Both point
  ;; at the transaction page instead. The owner template neither claims nor
  ;; denies damage for the same reason: cancel-from-returned may follow a
  ;; damage report, the other four do not.
  ;;
  ;; If per-situation copy is ever wanted, it IS expressible - `eq` is a
  ;; supported helper (see the unitType branches in booking-declined-request)
  ;; and transaction.last-transition is available - so a single template
  ;; could branch. That was weighed and rejected on the translation cost
  ;; above, NOT ruled out. Do not "simplify" this into one entry per
  ;; transition, though: :to cannot address both roles at once, and the two
  ;; roles are not in the same position (one is getting money back, the
  ;; other is not getting paid), so they must not share a template.
  ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------

  {:name :notification/booking-cancelled-to-customer,
   :on :transition/cancel,
   :to :actor.role/customer,
   :template :booking-cancelled}
  {:name :notification/booking-cancelled-to-provider,
   :on :transition/cancel,
   :to :actor.role/provider,
   :template :booking-cancelled-owner}
  ;; NEW (2026-10-13), alongside :transition/customer-cancel itself. SAME TWO ROLES as every
  ;; other cancel above, but DELIBERATELY NOT the same two templates. booking-cancelled-owner
  ;; says plainly "was cancelled by {marketplaceName}" - true for every operator cancel, and a
  ;; FALSE attribution here: the renter cancelled, the marketplace did not act. So this pair
  ;; gets its own templates (booking-customer-cancelled / booking-customer-cancelled-owner)
  ;; rather than reusing booking-cancelled/-owner, even though the three actions on
  ;; :transition/customer-cancel are byte-identical to :transition/cancel's. Money and attribution
  ;; are different facts, and only one of them is shared.
  {:name :notification/booking-customer-cancelled-to-customer,
   :on :transition/customer-cancel,
   :to :actor.role/customer,
   :template :booking-customer-cancelled}
  {:name :notification/booking-customer-cancelled-to-provider,
   :on :transition/customer-cancel,
   :to :actor.role/provider,
   :template :booking-customer-cancelled-owner}
  ;; NEW (2026-10-15), alongside :transition/cancel-from-accepted-on-laundry itself - same
  ;; two templates as :transition/cancel above (situation-neutral copy, see "TEN ENTRIES, TWO
  ;; TEMPLATES" note above - this is now effectively an eleventh and twelfth transition sharing
  ;; the same two).
  {:name :notification/booking-cancelled-from-accepted-on-laundry-to-customer,
   :on :transition/cancel-from-accepted-on-laundry,
   :to :actor.role/customer,
   :template :booking-cancelled}
  {:name :notification/booking-cancelled-from-accepted-on-laundry-to-provider,
   :on :transition/cancel-from-accepted-on-laundry,
   :to :actor.role/provider,
   :template :booking-cancelled-owner}
  ;; NEW (2026-10-15), alongside :transition/customer-cancel-from-accepted-on-laundry itself.
  ;; SAME TWO TEMPLATES as :transition/customer-cancel's own pair above - this is the same
  ;; event (the renter self-cancelled, attribution is theirs not the marketplace's), just
  ;; reachable from the second state the 2026-10-15 split created. No new copy needed.
  {:name :notification/booking-customer-cancelled-from-accepted-on-laundry-to-customer,
   :on :transition/customer-cancel-from-accepted-on-laundry,
   :to :actor.role/customer,
